WEBVTT

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.600
Do you hear?  Hooves beating

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.750
A carriage goes through the gentle hills

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.980
Someone comes to pick me up

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:45.950
And takes me

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.900
To the avenue with white flowers

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.050
To the home of wind

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.400
He takes me

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
I'm sure

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.750
Two weeks before Christmas,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:03.540
Anne and her friends were fervently
practicing for the concert.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.180
OH! Your face is full of splendor.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.760
You brighten the earth like rays of sunlight.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.910
And your gentle heart is loved
and respected by everyone.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:19.450
Fairy Queen, today is your birthday.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.440
Your servants will...

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.260
hold a grand banquet to celebrate this day.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.420
No, no!

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.700
What's wrong, Anne?

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:28.570
What?

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:29.860
Did I do something strange?

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.470
It's your steps. Why do walk like that?

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:33.720
Oh, this?

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.910
But I have no choice,

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:40.000
because I borrowed this shoes from Ruby.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.930
But they're two sizes too big.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.500
Look. If I walk normally they make noise.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.540
You look like the Fairy Grandmother.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.830
Try to see if you can fly.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.030
This is no laughing matter, Tillie.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.870
The success of the
play can be ruined because of me.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.760
Anne, if you can't walk with those shoes,
why don't you let Diana do your role?

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.180
You're not so good either,

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.130
so don't criticize me.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.120
Anne.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.280
I have an idea.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.440
Is that a ribbon tape?

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:18.460
That's right.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:22.440
If you tie this on your foot and shoes it'll
become a pair of good looking dancing shoes.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.520
Oh, Diana, that's a great idea!

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.140
That won't be enough,

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.570
you should also put a
handkerchief inside the shoes.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.020
I will do that too!

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.960
I'll use both methods.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.220
But it's unhygienic to put
a handkerchief inside the shoes.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:37.520
We can't care about that right now.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:43.630
To make the play a success,
I'll would even drink the most awful soup!

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.510
Look. It's almost 5.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:26.820
Oh, you're right!

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.610
I promised my mom that I would
be back by 5, I have to go now.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:33.720
That's right.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.190
Anne, we're going home.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.560
Well? Look, we'll succeed this time.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:50.760
Your servants will hold a grand
banquet to celebrate this day.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.470
Well? Now everything is all right.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.590
Those ribbon tapes are great.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.420
But it'll be weird that only
one of us uses ribbon tapes.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.670
Matthew, who was watched them shyly suddenly

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:00.420
It'll be all right.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.440
Matthew, who was watched them shyly suddenly

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.720
became conscious that there was something
about Anne different from her mates.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.680
Anne had a brighter
face, and bigger, starrier eyes,

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:13.010
and more delicate features than the others.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:17.530
But the difference that disturbed him
did not consist in any of these respects.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:21.880
Then, good bye, Anne.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.200
I'll see you all off.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.010
Really?

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.330
Matthew, are you home?

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:53.400
What are you doing there?

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:59.860
No, nothing... I was just resting here...

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:03.020
And why do you still have one boot on?

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:04.240
Boot?

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:05.870
Well... this...

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.360
Well now, but it really is nothing.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:22.210
Sophia finally asked why she
has to take on the role of a boy.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.540
So I said to her:

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:30.200
We have an important motive.
To gain money for a school flag.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.560
We must all work together and do our best.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.850
And today, Ruby lent me her slippers,
but they are too big.

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:39.260
At dinner time

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:39.700
Ruby's feet are much bigger...

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:44.810
Matthew took to pondering over what
made Anne different from the others,

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:48.320
but he didn't found the answer.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:06.280
He could not refer it to Marilla.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:11.210
He had recourse to his pipe that evening to
help him study it out, much to Marilla's disgust.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.610
That's the practice of a painful groan.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:21.910
Even the wolves would get
scared to listen such sounds.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:47.550
I see, so that's it.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:50.590
Did you just say something?

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.890
No... I didn't say anything.

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:57.280
The more Matthew thought about the matter

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:04.220
the more he was convinced that Anne
never had been dressed like the other girls.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:07.590
The sleeves...

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:14.770
If Matthew knew there was such a thing
as fashion in dress it was as much as he did,

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:18.200
but ever since Anne had come to Green Gables,

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:22.580
she wore plain, dark dresses,
all made after the same unvarying pattern.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:30.250
All the other girls wear clothes with
fresh colors and puffed sleeves.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:43.370
Matthew, you're very strange today.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:45.630
W-Well now...

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:50.030
What's wrong?
You're mumbling to yourself.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:53.520
N-Nothing. Everything's fine.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.490
I was just thinking about something.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:03.020
I'm going to bed.

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:32.280
But why does Marilla alway keep her
so plainly and soberly gowned?

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:38.480
Of course, it must be all right.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:45.210
Marilla knows best and Marilla is
bringing her up.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:52.110
But surely one dress will do no harm.

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:16.090
The very next evening Matthew betook
himself to Carmody without telling Marilla.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.970
He went to buy a dress for Anne like the
ones Diana and the other girls wear.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:25.900
That would be a great Christmas present.

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:30.380
Just thinking about that made Matthew rejoice.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:40.530
He always had gone to William Blair's store,
but today he didn't.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:45.600
William Blair's two daughters frequently
waited on customers there.

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:51.260
Today the matter required
explanation and consultation,

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:55.140
and Matthew held them in absolute dread.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:59.100
In Samuel Lawson's store

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.550
Samuel himself or
his son would wait on him.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:06.250
So Matthew decided to go
to Samuel Lawson's store.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:39.450
Oh! Please, excuse me.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.720
Good evening, Mr. Cuthbert... Am I right?

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.520
What can I do for you?

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:53.780
Alas! Matthew did not know that Samuel,
in the recent expansion of his business

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:59.410
had set up a lady clerk named Miss Harris.

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:02.690
Well... What would you like?

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:10.070
Have you any... any... any... well now...

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:14.450
...any garden rakes?

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:16.640
Garden rakes? In this season?

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:18.650
No... Well...

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:22.400
I believe we have one or two left over.

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:25.560
But they're upstairs in
the lumber room. I'll go and see.

00:12:43.450 --> 00:12:46.000
Mr. Cuthbert, here you go.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:49.160
This was the last one.

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:51.200
Because it's already Winter.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:53.200
R-Right.

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:55.650
Anything else today?

00:12:55.720 --> 00:13:00.570
Well now, since you
suggest it, I might as well take...

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.780
No... That is... look at...

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:06.570
No... Buy...

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:10.400
Well... I want to buy...

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:15.860
Well... Some hayseed.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:17.560
Hayseed?

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:21.390
Please, stop joking, Mr. Cuthbert!

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:23.250
J-Joking?

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:25.410
You clearly have to be!

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:28.250
You know that we
only keep hayseed in the spring,

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:29.510
don't you?

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:33.640
Oh, certainly, certainly

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:36.230
just as you say.

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:40.710
Mr. Cuthbert, the money for the rake?

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:42.320
And your hat.

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:44.370
My apologies!

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:48.640
Well... well... the weather is too hot...

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:53.120
Thank you for coming.
Let me count your change.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.360
Anything else?

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:10.050
Well now... if it isn't too much trouble
I might as well... that is...

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:12.990
I'd like to look at... at...

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:15.800
Could I see that?

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:23.560
I understand.

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:25.410
Sugar isn't it?

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:29.220
R-Right...

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:33.480
It's the only kind we have.
How much can I get you?

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:34.700
Well...

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.160
I'll take twenty pounds of it.

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:43.990
Why did he felt so pressed
to buy a rake and sugar?

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:50.970
Matthew had driven halfway home
before he was his own man again.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:56.400
But it served him right, he thought,
for going to a strange store.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:00.960
But now the mistake was already done.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:14.320
Did Matthew go somewhere?

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.050
Where did you go?

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:26.410
Well... I-I... just went to Carmody.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:29.510
What is this?

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:33.090
Well... I went shop a little.

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:35.450
What did you buy?

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:38.810
Well... Just some brown sugar.

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:40.540
Brown sugar!?

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:43.160
And you call this, just some?

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:50.600
You know I never use it except for the
hired man's porridge or black fruit cake.

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:55.620
Jerry's gone and I've
made my cake long ago.

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:58.360
My, it's not good sugar, either.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:02.490
Take a look. It's coarse and dark.

00:16:03.670 --> 00:16:07.050
William Blair doesn't usually
keep sugar like that.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:11.360
I thought it might come in handy sometime.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:18.100
Whatever were you thinking, Matthew?

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:27.170
Matthew's plan to secretly buy
a dress with puffed sleeves for Anne,

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:30.100
somehow wasn't discovered by Marilla.

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:32.400
When Matthew came to think the matter over

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:36.770
he decided that a woman was
required to cope with the situation.

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:39.160
There was only Mrs. Lynde,

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:44.860
for of no other woman in Avonlea would
Matthew have dared to ask advice.

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:54.210
And that good lady promptly took
the matter out of the harassed man's hands.

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:58.170
To be sure I will.
I'll Pick out a dress for you to give.

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:03.270
I'm going to
Carmody tomorrow and I'll attend to it.

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:05.630
Have you something particular in mind?

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:08.860
It doesn't matter if you don't.

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:11.870
I'll just go by my own judgment then.

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:13.180
Is that all right?

00:17:13.330 --> 00:17:15.660
W-Well now...

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:19.930
I believe a nice
rich brown would just suit Anne.

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:24.470
Right. And William Blair has some new gloria
in that's real pretty.

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:27.720
Perhaps you'd like me to make it up for her, too.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:31.910
Seeing that if Marilla was to make
it Anne would probably get wind of it

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:35.050
before the time and spoil the surprise?

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:38.570
No, it isn't a mite of trouble.

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.350
I'll make it to fit my niece, Jenny Gillis,

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:43.070
for they are as like as
two peas as far as figure goes.

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:46.690
Well now, I'm much obliged.

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:48.990
Only... Well...

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.060
Only...?

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:51.820
I mean... Well...

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:53.950
How should I explain?

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:59.710
I think they make the sleeves
different nowadays to what they used to be.

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:03.600
My... Matthew is interested in fashionable clothes?

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:05.000
No... Well...

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:06.290
Actually...

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:08.330
Of course.

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:11.260
You needn't worry a speck more about it.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.210
I'll make it up in the very latest fashion.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.830
And... the sleeves...

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:20.470
I understand. I'll make them like that.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:24.460
For Anne, I'll do my best.

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:28.630
I'll be glad to see Anne wearing
something decent for once.

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:30.750
I suppose Marilla's trying to cultivate

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:35.360
a spirit of humility in Anne by
dressing her as she does,

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:39.900
but it's more likely to cultivate
envy and discontent.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.220
Mrs. Rachel Lynde

00:18:57.220 --> 00:19:02.500
went to Carmody and bought the materials
at William Blair's store like she promised.

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:13.460
She started sewing as soon has she
got home, to finish it in the shortest time.

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:24.330
Meanwhile, Anne was
busy practicing for the concert,

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:27.500
and didn't find out about Matthew's plan.

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:35.400
And every night she would torment
Matthew and Marilla with her proud groans.

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:39.930
Marilla knew all the following fortnight
that Matthew had something on his mind,

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:43.450
but what it was she could not guess.

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:47.460
Two weeks passed,

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:51.380
and it was the Christmas Eve
that Matthew longed for.

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:04.780
Matthew.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:06.580
I finished, Matthew!

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:08.590
Oh, Rachel, what brings you today?

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:12.460
Well, Matthew
asked me to make a dress for Anne.

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:14.930
Matthew asked? A dress?

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:17.290
Well, anyway, you'd better come in first.

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:19.210
I won't stand on ceremony then.

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:32.360
It was because of this.

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:34.190
If you had done the dress

00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:38.700
Anne could find out about it
and the Christmas present would be ruined.

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:41.810
So Matthew requested me.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:45.180
So this is what Matthew
has been looking so mysterious over

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:48.530
and grinning about
to himself for two weeks, is it?

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:54.360
I knew he was up to some foolishness.

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:59.960
Well, I must say I don't
think Anne needed any more dresses.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:01.380
But, Marilla...

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:07.370
I made her three
good, warm, serviceable ones this fall,

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:09.630
and anything more is sheer extravagance.

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:14.380
My, my, there's enough material
in those sleeves alone to make a waist.

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:21.500
Rachel, how much do I owe
you for the material and sewing?

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:22.180
Matthew!

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:26.910
You'll just pamper Anne's vanity!

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:31.170
And she's as vain as a peacock now.

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:34.100
But Marilla, the clothes Anne wears now...

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:37.530
Well, I hope she'll be satisfied at last,

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:44.640
for I know she's been hankering after
those silly sleeves ever since they came in.

00:21:44.970 --> 00:21:49.310
Although she never said a word after the first.

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:55.360
The puffs have been getting
bigger and more ridiculous right along.

00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:58.450
They're as big as balloons now.

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:04.870
Next year anybody who wears them
will have to go through a door sideways.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.870
Marilla, you are exaggerating a bi...

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:10.820
Anne is back, Marilla.

00:22:12.280 --> 00:22:13.160
You're right.

00:22:15.250 --> 00:22:17.320
Matthew, what are you doing there?

00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:20.330
Will you let your surprise be ruined?

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:22.710
W-well now...

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:23.520
Hurry!

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:29.930
I'm home, Marilla.

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:31.350
Welcome back.

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:33.340
Good afternoon, Mrs. Lynde.

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:34.740
Good afternoon.

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:36.980
Wasn't Matthew at home right now?

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:38.030
I'm not sure.

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:41.920
We've practiced the final parts in school.

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:48.730
It was a quiet Christmas Eve.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:52.510
There wasn't much celebration
going on in the Cuthbert's household.

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:55.740
But there was an air of warmth in the kitchen.

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:04.400
Anne never stopped talking
about the concert to be held tomorrow.

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:08.760
Marilla did not interfere
and listened everything.

00:23:10.870 --> 00:23:17.210
And Matthew smiled and imagined
Anne's expression when she's see his present.

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:23.060
When Anne went to sleep,
he sat and smoke his pipe.

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:31.830
Please open the window
a little to let the air go through.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:34.010
Right.

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:39.860
It's snowing.

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:41.800
It's snowing?

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:50.130
Looks like it'll be a White Christmas tomorrow.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:52.210
Yes.

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:23.630
Anne was struck speechless
when she received Matthew's gift.

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.810
Next episode of Anne of Green Gables:

00:24:27.090 --> 00:24:28.740
The Christmas Concert

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:30.110
Please watch!
